34,2,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears our history is one of selective durability. While some structural elements hold, our greenery has suffered a tragic lack of foresight regarding aftercare. A beautiful drawing is little comfort to a dead tree.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The earth-moving options are limited. Only two firms are tendering, and both have warned about clay subsoil and slippage risks. However, smaller-scale work seems more straightforward and reasonably priced.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Mio,,"Oh wow, the costs are scary! It costs three times as much to maintain plants and water features as it does for hard surfaces, and that gap is just getting bigger every year!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The staffing situation is critical. The internal groundskeeping roll has collapsed from eighteen posts to just seven, leaving us heavily reliant on bought-in services and a few dedicated volunteer groups.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The park is under significant stress. Drainage callouts have doubled since the culvert was capped, and August heat readings are notably higher than nearby streets. Furthermore, most users stick to firm ground to reach the tram.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, if the internal staff has collapsed, it explains why the eastern avenue planting failed so miserably. We cannot commission a lush paradise if there is no one left to water it.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Minako, the drainage issues you mentioned align with the clay subsoil risks the firms flagged. Any plan requiring deep excavation or heavy ground-shifting could be a liability.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@Asuka, it's so sad about the staff! If we only have seven people left, we definitely can't afford a plan that needs a ton of fancy gardening or expensive specialist care.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, exactly. We must be realistic. Relying on a handful of staff and a few volunteers is a fragile strategy. Any plan requiring intensive, daily manual upkeep is destined for failure.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Misaki, the clay subsoil and drainage failures suggest that we should avoid any design that disrupts the natural water flow or requires complex underground infrastructure. We need a plan that works with the land, not against it.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, I quite agree. Given that our previous ""handsome"" openings often lead to short lives, we should prioritize structural simplicity over aesthetic ambition. A plan that demands constant intervention is merely a future ruin.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, I agree. Furthermore, the depot notes indicate that only the southern area can be hoarded in stages. Any plan requiring a total site shutdown or massive central excavation will likely face severe delays.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Mio,,"@Misaki, that's a great point! If we can't close the whole park, we should pick something that doesn't need a giant construction zone. Plus, it'll save us money on those expensive hoarding costs!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Misaki, that is a logical constraint. If we combine the staffing shortage with the construction limitations, we must avoid any plan that is both labor-intensive to maintain and disruptive to build.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, I agree. We must also consider the users. Since most people use the park as a transit route to the tram, any plan that obstructs those firm, open paths will likely be ignored or worn down quickly.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are in a rare moment of alignment. We require a design that is structurally modest, avoids deep excavation in clay, and respects our depleted staffing. Let us see which candidate survives these realities.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Misaki,,I agree. We need a plan that is realistic about the ground conditions and the limited number of firms available to do the work. Simplicity in both construction and maintenance is the only way to ensure delivery.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Mio,,I'm totally on board! Let's find a plan that's easy on the budget and doesn't need a huge army of gardeners to keep it from becoming a jungle. I can't wait to see the options!,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"I concur. We must prioritize a plan that is operationally sustainable. If the maintenance burden exceeds our current capacity, the project will fail regardless of its initial design.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Minako,,I agree. We must ensure the final choice respects the physical behavior of the site and the people who use it. A plan that ignores drainage and transit patterns is merely a paper exercise.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Mio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
